issues.body:
On Android 11+ realm `portMapping` never maps anything:

```
WARN	realm port mapping failed; continuing without it	{"error": "gateway discovery failed: no NAT found"}
```

Android 11+ denies apps `bind()` on a netlink route socket and `RTM_GETLINK`, so `net.Interfaces()` fails (same cause as golang/go#40569; a small test program on the test phone prints `route ip+net: netlinkrib: permission denied`). Every go-nat discovery path runs into it, and go-nat drops the errors, so only `no NAT found` is logged. Past discovery, `upnp_NAT.GetInternalAddress` calls `net.Interfaces()` again and `AddPortMapping` returns `(0, nil)` when that fails.

`portmap_android.go` keeps `nat.DiscoverGateway` when `net.Interfaces()` works (NAT-PMP included). Otherwise it does the UPnP part itself: multicast IPv4 addresses from `wlynxg/anet` (already linked through pion/transport), SSDP through goupnp's `httpu`/`ssdp`, `DeviceByURLCtx`, and a small `nat.NAT` that takes the internal address from the socket the SSDP reply arrived on. Port selection copies go-nat's. Other platforms build `portmap_other.go` with the old call. anet needs cgo for the API level, which the android build in `hyperbole.py` has.

Tested on Android 16 in Termux, MiniUPnPd 1.8 router (port-restricted), server behind a symmetric NAT (the server is Xray-core's realm implementation, same protocol). v2.13.0 logs `no NAT found` and the punch times out. This PR maps the port (`UPNP (IG1-IP1)`, 2.2 s), punches in 126 ms and connects. On Windows both builds behave the same.

<details><summary>v2.13.0</summary>
<pre><code>realm port mapping started	{"port": 60905}
realm port mapping failed; continuing without it	{"error": "gateway discovery failed: no NAT found"}
realm client STUN discovery completed	{"addresses": ["198.51.100.7:60905"], "duration": "261ms"}
realm client connect request started	{"addresses": ["198.51.100.7:60905"]}
realm client punch started	{"candidates": ["203.0.113.5:22869", "203.0.113.5:22904", "203.0.113.5:22905"]}
failed to initialize client	{"error": "invalid config: realm.punch: punch timed out"}
</code></pre>
</details>

<details><summary>this PR</summary>
<pre><code>realm port mapping started	{"port": 33719}
realm port mapping added	{"gateway": "UPNP (IG1-IP1)", "port": 33719, "external": "198.51.100.7:40845", "duration": "2.242s"}
realm client STUN discovery completed	{"addresses": ["198.51.100.7:33719"], "duration": "242ms"}
realm client connect request started	{"addresses": ["198.51.100.7:33719", "198.51.100.7:40845"]}
realm client punch started	{"candidates": ["203.0.113.5:22849", "203.0.113.5:22904", "203.0.113.5:22905"]}
realm client punch completed	{"peer": "203.0.113.5:22851", "packet": "hello", "duration": "126ms"}
connected to server	{"addr": "203.0.113.5:22851", "udpEnabled": true}
</code></pre>
</details>

The peer's hello comes from port 22851, which isn't one of its candidates. The port-restricted router drops that on the STUN-mapped port, so it most likely came in through the UPnP mapping.

Limits:
- NAT-PMP still fails on Android 11+: go-netroute binds a netlink route socket
- without a UPnP gateway the fallback gives up after about 4.2 s instead of right away: two SSDP searches, each waits MX 2 s + 100 ms (from the code, not measured)
- a send error on any one multicast interface fails the whole search (goupnp's `MultiClient` uses an errgroup), as goupnp does on other platforms
- go-nat's generic IGD path (`ssdp:all`) isn't ported
- untested: Android 10 and older, IGD2 routers, renewal, clients inside a VpnService

Builds for android/arm64 (NDK, cgo), linux and windows; `go list -deps ./app` is unchanged on all three. Android binary +28 KiB, windows +512 B.

go-nat could run the same discovery itself (goupnp's `DiscoverDevicesCtx` takes no address list). I reported it there as libp2p/go-nat#45 and can send the change there too; if you'd rather have the fix only in go-nat, let me know. Until it lands, this keeps the workaround in one Android-only fi
